expels() -> spoteffects() -> dotrap() -> domagictrap() -> tamedog()

That last routine replaces the monster with a substitute which has
extra room to record pet info.  Unfortunately if the affected monster
is the one which is currently moving, expels() and the routines above
it dealing with this monster's movement don't know that it has been
replaced and continue to use the old pointer for it.  In your case
that resulted in an eventual "monsndx: can't index monster" panic,
but it could corrupt memory and lead to even more mysterious trouble.


